<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Bulgaria</h1><p class="st-meta">1878 CE – 1908 CE · capital Sofia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Autonomous Bulgarian principality created after the Russo-Turkish War, which united with Eastern Rumelia and proclaimed full independence in 1908.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia's</a> victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-stefano">Treaty of San Stefano</a> in March 1878, which created a large <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> reduced it to an autonomous principality between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the Balkan mountains, under Ottoman suzerainty. In 1879 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarnovo-constitution">Tarnovo Constitution</a> was adopted and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-battenberg">Alexander of Battenberg</a> elected prince, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarnovo-constitution">Tarnovo Constitution</a> created a parliament elected by male suffrage, though princes and strong premiers often ruled forcefully. Unification with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-rumelia">Eastern Rumelia</a> in 1885 was defended by victory over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-slivnitsa">Slivnitsa</a>. Russian anger forced Alexander's abdication in 1886, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-bulgaria">Ferdinand of Saxe-Coburg</a> was chosen in 1887, while premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-stambolov">Stefan Stambolov</a> built a modern state and army.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 5 October 1908, during the crisis over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, Ferdinand proclaimed full independence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> and took the title of tsar, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bulgaria">Kingdom of Bulgaria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarnovo-constitution">Tarnovo Constitution</a> remained in force until 1947.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Principality of Bulgaria" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bulgaria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1878 CE – 1908 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sofia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alexander of Battenberg, Ferdinand I, Stefan Stambolov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Bulgarian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bulgarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Knyazhestvo Balgariya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=principality-of-bulgaria" aria-label="Principality of Bulgaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5244 660 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Bulgaria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5564 916L5709 970M5564 916L5601 934M5564 916L5564 916"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5601 934h0M5564 916h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5564 916h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5564 916h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sofia. <a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=principality-of-bulgaria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
